<h2 class="tex-section" id="section:truncated-object-of-infinity"><span data-tag="05EA">4.7</span> Truncated Objects and Higher Homotopy Categories</h2>
<p>
Let $n$ be an integer. Recall that a Kan complex $X$ is <em>$n$-truncated</em> if, for every integer $m \geq n+2$, every morphism $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \rightarrow X$ can be extended to an $m$-simplex of $X$. Using the morphism spaces introduced in §<a data-tag="01J3" href="/tag/01J3">4.6</a>, we can formulate a counterpart of this condition for objects of an arbitrary $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We say that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>$n$-truncated</em> if, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is an $n$-truncated Kan complex (Definition <a data-tag="05EC" href="/tag/05EC">4.7.1.1</a>). In §<a data-tag="05EB" href="/tag/05EB">4.7.1</a>, we summarize the formal properties of this notion and show that it can be reformulated as a filling condition (Proposition <a data-tag="05ET" href="/tag/05ET">4.7.1.16</a>). We will be particularly interested in the cases where $n \leq 0$, which we study in §<a data-tag="02H6" href="/tag/02H6">4.7.3</a> and §<a data-tag="04UX" href="/tag/04UX">4.7.4</a>: 
</p>
<ul>
<li><p>
For $n \leq -2$, an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is <em>final</em>: that is, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is contractible (Definition <a data-tag="02H7" href="/tag/02H7">4.7.3.1</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(-1)$-truncated if and only if it is <em>subterminal</em>: that is, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is either empty or contractible (Definition <a data-tag="04UY" href="/tag/04UY">4.7.4.1</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $0$-truncated if and only if it is <em>discrete</em>: that is, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is a disjoint union of contractible Kan complexes (Definition <a data-tag="05EY" href="/tag/05EY">4.7.4.13</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
The preceding definition has a relative counterpart, which we study in §<a data-tag="05F8" href="/tag/05F8">4.7.2</a>. We say that a morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$ in an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>$n$-truncated</em> if, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, precomposition with $f$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,Y)$ (Definition <a data-tag="05F9" href="/tag/05F9">4.7.2.1</a>), or equivalently if $f$ is $n$-truncated when viewed as an object of the slice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="05FF" href="/tag/05FF">4.7.2.7</a>). For $n \leq -2$, this is equivalent to the condition that $f$ is an isomorphism (Example <a data-tag="05FD" href="/tag/05FD">4.7.2.5</a>). In the case $n = -1$, it is equivalent to the condition that $f$ is a <em>monomorphism</em>, which we study in §<a data-tag="04VD" href="/tag/04VD">4.7.5</a> (Definition <a data-tag="04VE" href="/tag/04VE">4.7.5.1</a>). 
</p>
<p>
In §<a data-tag="0583" href="/tag/0583">4.7.6</a>, we study $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ in which every object is $n$-truncated: that is, the morphism spaces $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)$ are $n$-truncated for every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If this condition is satisfied, we will say that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>locally $n$-truncated</em> (Definition <a data-tag="0584" href="/tag/0584">4.7.6.1</a>). The case $n = 0$ is already familiar: an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $0$-truncated if and only if it is equivalent to (the nerve of) its homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ (Corollary <a data-tag="06RE" href="/tag/06RE">4.7.4.23</a>). The main goal of this section is to obtain an analogous characterization of locally $n$-truncated $\infty $-categories for other values of $n$. For this, we require a brief digression. Recall that a simplicial set $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is an <em>$\infty $-category</em> if, for every pair of integers $0 &lt; i &lt; m$, every inner horn $\sigma _0: \Lambda ^{m}_{i} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ can be extended to an $m$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ (Definition <a data-tag="003A" href="/tag/003A">1.4.0.1</a>). In this case, $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is (isomorphic to the nerve of) an ordinary category if and only if the extension $\sigma $ is always unique (Proposition <a data-tag="0031" href="/tag/0031">1.3.4.1</a>). In §<a data-tag="057D" href="/tag/057D">4.7.7</a> we study a more general situation, where the extension $\sigma $ is unique for $m &gt; n$, where $n$ is some fixed positive integer. If this condition is satisfied, we say that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an <em>$n$-category</em> (Definition <a data-tag="057E" href="/tag/057E">4.7.7.1</a>). It is not difficult to show that every $n$-category is locally $(n-1)$-truncated (Proposition <a data-tag="057Y" href="/tag/057Y">4.7.7.20</a>). Conversely, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a locally $(n-1)$-truncated $\infty $-category, then there exists an equivalence $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is an $n$-category. We will give two proofs of this result: 
</p>
<ul>
<li><p>
In §<a data-tag="058P" href="/tag/058P">4.7.8</a>, we show that a locally $(n-1)$-truncated $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an $n$-category if and only if it is minimal in dimensions $\geq n$ (Proposition <a data-tag="058Q" href="/tag/058Q">4.7.8.1</a>). In particular, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $(n-1)$-truncated and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is a minimal model of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is an $n$-category. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
In §<a data-tag="0596" href="/tag/0596">4.7.9</a>, we associate to every $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ an $n$-category $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$, which we call the <em>homotopy $n$-category of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$</em> (Construction <a data-tag="059F" href="/tag/059F">4.7.9.9</a>). The homotopy $n$-category $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$ is equipped with a comparison functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$, which is an equivalence if and only if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $(n-1)$-truncated (Corollary <a data-tag="059P" href="/tag/059P">4.7.9.16</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06QS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QS">Remark <span data-tag="06QS">4.7.0.1</span> <span class="named">(Towers of Higher Homotopy Categories)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. The homotopy $n$-categories $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$ constructed in §<a data-tag="0596" href="/tag/0596">4.7.9</a> can be arranged into an inverse system </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}4}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}3}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}2}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}1}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}} = \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} ) \]
</div>
<p> where the transition functors are isofibrations (Corollary <a data-tag="059P" href="/tag/059P">4.7.9.16</a>). Moreover, the natural maps $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$ are bijective on simplices of dimension $&lt; n$ (Remark <a data-tag="059G" href="/tag/059G">4.7.9.10</a>). It follows that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be identified with the inverse limit $\varprojlim _{n} \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$. In the special case where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= X$ is a Kan complex, this construction recovers the canonical Postnikov tower </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \rightarrow \pi _{\leq 4}(X) \rightarrow \pi _{\leq 3}(X) \rightarrow \pi _{\leq 2}(X) \rightarrow \pi _{\leq 1}(X) \]
</div>
<p> of Example <a data-tag="055M" href="/tag/055M">3.5.8.2</a>. See Example <a data-tag="059C" href="/tag/059C">4.7.9.6</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
